Cite    : Wiatrak et al., "BacBench: Evaluating Genomic Language Models for Bacteria".

This file is a byte-identical, unmodified copy of BacBench's gene-essentiality probe
script. It documents the task's scoring protocol: per-genome AUROC / AUPRC
(`calculate_metrics_per_genome`) of a linear probe trained on frozen gene embeddings.

It is a reference only and is never imported or executed by gLMBench: it depends on
`torch` / `pytorch_lightning` / `torchmetrics`, and its metric is entangled with a
PyTorch-Lightning training loop. gLMBench re-implements the protocol, torch-free, in
`glmbench.tasks.bacbench_essentiality_metrics`, with two deviations from this reference:

  1. Probe: this reference trains a torch `LayerNorm -> Dropout -> Linear` net (AdamW, BCE,
     up to 100 epochs, early stopping on validation AUROC). gLMBench fits a CPU scikit-learn
     LogisticRegression on standardized embeddings. Scores therefore reproduce the
     published protocol, not its exact numbers.
  2. Metric: this reference uses `torchmetrics.functional.auroc/average_precision`.
     gLMBench uses `sklearn.metrics.roc_auc_score/average_precision_score`, which are
     numerically equivalent for binary labels. An optional test (`tests/unit/test_phase9.py`,
     skipped unless `torchmetrics` is installed) checks they agree to 1e-6 on the fixture.

Aggregation across genomes: the reference reports both the mean and the median across test
genomes, and the BacBench paper's tables use the mean. gLMBench records both and sets
`primary_metric` to the macro-mean AUROC.

Rules. Copy, never edit. The sha256 in `SHA256SUMS` is checked by a unit test, so an edit
to the copy fails loudly.
